\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The chapter dependency map. Each box is a chapter number; an arrow means ``read this first''. Inside a part, read in order. The long arrows show the few places where a later part leans on a specific earlier chapter: the linear algebra of \cref {ch:linalg} under the tensor arithmetic of \cref {ch:shapes}, the local detectors of \cref {ch:localrx} under the residual Mahalanobis score of \cref {ch:residualmaha}, and the fine-tuning machinery of \cref {ch:finetune} under the experiment chapters. Part X leans on the namer (\cref {ch:namer}) and, through it, on the reconstruction, masking, spectral-stem and evaluation chapters.\relax }}{xliv}{figure.caption.11}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Reconstruct, then subtract. Blocks of the observed cube are hidden and the model, trained only on ordinary scenes, redraws them from their surroundings. Subtracting the reconstruction from the observation, band by band, gives a per-pixel error map; where the redrawing is most wrong (amber) is where something unusual is. The amber square stands for an object the model has never seen. (Course figure, Indradhanu part 1.)\relax }}{7}{figure.caption.22}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces A Gaussian spectral response function with FWHM $10$ nm, drawn from \cref {eq:light:gauss}. The response falls to one half exactly $5$ nm either side of the centre; the three amber points are the samples used in \cref {we:light:srf}.\relax }}{17}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces The mean reflectance of each of the $165$ common-grid bands over the normalisation corpus (teal line), with one standard deviation either side (shaded). Real numbers from \texttt {app/constants/hyperspectral.json}, plotted at the grid wavelengths; the four breaks are the atmospheric windows removed from the grid.\relax }}{19}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces From photograph to spectrum. (a) A colour photograph stores three bands per pixel. (b) A hyperspectral cube stores one image per wavelength, with shape \texttt {(C, H, W)}. (c) Reading one pixel down the band axis gives its spectrum. (Course figure, Indradhanu part 2; the curve is illustrative.)\relax }}{19}{figure.caption.28}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces The common grid drawn to scale. Ticks are the $200$ ladder rungs from $460$ to $2{,}450$ nm; grey rungs fall inside the hatched atmospheric windows and are removed ($6 + 2 + 11 + 16 = 35$), teal rungs are kept. Braces give the number of kept bands in each of the five segments ($46 + 16 + 19 + 34 + 50 = 165$). The window $0$--$450$ nm lies below the ladder and removes nothing.\relax }}{32}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces The same grid as drawn in the Indradhanu course (part 3): a ladder of $200$ wavelengths loses $35$ rungs to four atmospheric windows, leaving $165$ bands in five segments of $46$, $16$, $19$, $34$ and $50$.\relax }}{32}{figure.caption.37}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces The shipped Action graph, edges read from each Action type's declared inputs. Each box writes its outputs (monospace) to its own folder. \texttt {anomaly\_scoring} has two possible parents besides the optional annotation, so the structure is a graph, not a chain; Indradhanu is one model ticked \emph {inside} \texttt {anomaly\_scoring}. Export re-reads the raw scene file to recover map coordinates.\relax }}{38}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces The same chain as drawn in the Indradhanu course (part 5), emphasising that Indradhanu is one option inside the \texttt {anomaly\_scoring} Action, not an Action of its own.\relax }}{38}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces The hyperspectral vendable as a data structure: two arrays of identical shape (the reflectance cube and its validity cube) and five lists with one entry per band. There is no field for map coordinates.\relax }}{43}{figure.caption.44}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces How a vendable reaches Indradhanu (course figure, Indradhanu part 4). Of the filtered vendable's fields the model uses the cube and the validity cube; a pixel is spatially valid if it is valid in at least one band.\relax }}{44}{figure.caption.45}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Resampling one spectrum onto the grid (\cref {we:bandpipeline:resample}). Native samples (teal) at $1{,}003$, $1{,}011$, $1{,}019$, $1{,}027$ nm; grid values (amber) at $1{,}000$, $1{,}010$, $1{,}020$, $1{,}030$ nm, each read off the straight line between its two neighbours. Below the first sample the value is held constant; above the last, the code extends the last line.\relax }}{54}{figure.caption.47}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Why each band is normalised separately (course figure, Indradhanu part 9). (a) Raw reflectance puts a dim blue band and a bright near-infrared band on very different scales. (b) Subtracting each band's mean and dividing by its standard deviation puts every band on one scale, centred at $0$ with spread $1$. Distributions are illustrative.\relax }}{61}{figure.caption.48}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Projecting $\mathbf {x} = (3,1)$ onto the direction $\mathbf {u} = (1,1)$. The closest point of the line $t\mathbf {u}$ is $\mathbf {p} = 2\mathbf {u} = (2,2)$; the residual $\mathbf {r}$ meets the line at a right angle. The grey segments go to two other points of the line, which are further away.\relax }}{70}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Before and after whitening. (a) Sixty points drawn from a Gaussian with the covariance $\begin {psmallmatrix} 2 & 3 3 & 10\end {psmallmatrix}$ of \cref {we:linalg:cov} (grey; an illustrative sample, seed 0, \texttt {tools/compute\_linalg.py}) and the four worked pixels (teal). The ellipses are the points at whitened distance 1 and 2; the arrows are the eigenvectors scaled by the square roots of their eigenvalues, 3.32 and 1. (b) The same points after $\mathbf {z} = \mathbf {W}\mathbf {d}$: the ellipses have become circles, and the eigenvectors the coordinate axes.\relax }}{82}{figure.caption.51}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces The standard normal density. The light and dark tails together hold 1\% of the area (beyond $\pm 2.576$); the dark tails alone hold 0.1\% (beyond $\pm 3.291$). They are thin, so the right panel magnifies the grey box on the right tail. The thermal pixel of \cref {sec:rx:surprise} sits at $z = 3.42$, beyond both thresholds.\relax }}{87}{figure.caption.55}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces Chi-square densities for $C = 1, 2, 3, 10$ degrees of freedom. The right panel magnifies the grey box: its shaded tails hold 1\% of the area for $C = 3$ (beyond 11.34) and for $C = 10$ (beyond 23.21). As $C$ grows the whole distribution moves right (mean $C$) and spreads (standard deviation $\sqrt {2C}$), so a score of 15 is alarming in 3 dimensions and ordinary in 10.\relax }}{93}{figure.caption.63}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces A dual window with outer half-size $o = 4$ and inner half-size $i = 1$. The background is the $9\times 9$ outer square minus the $3\times 3$ guard: $81 - 9 = 72$ pixels. The product's defaults, $o = 25$ and $i = 5$, give a $51\times 51$ square and an $11\times 11$ guard.\relax }}{103}{figure.caption.70}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.1}{\ignorespaces The six-pixel signal of \cref {eq:recon:signal}, its leave-one-out predictions and the residuals, drawn to scale. The anomaly at pixel\nobreakspace {}4 gets the largest residual; its two neighbours get a halo of residual because their predictions used pixel\nobreakspace {}4.\relax }}{123}{figure.caption.76}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.2}{\ignorespaces Why the model must not see what it predicts. (a) Asked to reproduce what it can see, a model learns to copy; the residual is zero everywhere and nothing is detected. (b) A masked autoencoder hides about 65\% of the tokens (dashed) and is graded only on them; it must predict from context, so at the hidden anomaly (amber) it paints ordinary background and leaves a large error. (Course figure IW\nobreakspace {}6.1.)\relax }}{127}{figure.caption.79}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.3}{\ignorespaces The Indradhanu forward pass}}{130}{figure.caption.80}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.1}{\ignorespaces The two layouts of the same numbers. (a) Spatial \texttt {(B, C, H, W)}: a grid of positions per channel, as convolutions need. (b) \texttt {flatten(2)} joins the two spatial axes into $N = H\times W$ positions. (c) \texttt {transpose(1, 2)} makes each position a row of $C$ numbers, a token, as attention and LayerNorm need. A reshape alone would scramble the order. (Course figure IW\nobreakspace {}7.1.)\relax }}{135}{figure.caption.82}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.2}{\ignorespaces A $3\times 3$ kernel sliding with stride\nobreakspace {}2 over a $4\times 4$ input padded by one ring of zeros, the setting of encoder stages\nobreakspace {}2 to\nobreakspace {}4. Consecutive windows share a column (the overlap is $k-s=1$). The corner windows see only 4 real pixels; the bottom-right one sees 9.\relax }}{140}{figure.caption.83}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.3}{\ignorespaces A $1\times 1$ convolution is a matrix-vector product per pixel. At every pixel the 4-band vector $\mathbf {x}$ is multiplied by the same $2\times 4$ matrix $\mathbf {W}$, giving two output channels, $y_0 = 1.1$ and $y_1 = -0.5$ (the numbers of \cref {we:compressor:mix}). The spatial grid is untouched; only the channel axis changes. (Course figure IW\nobreakspace {}8.1.)\relax }}{144}{figure.caption.84}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.1}{\ignorespaces The spectral bottleneck around the SegFormer. A $1\times 1$ convolution with BatchNorm mixes each pixel's 165 normalised bands into 32 channels (5{,}376 parameters); the spatial model runs on those 32; a second $1\times 1$ convolution, without normalisation, expands them back to 165 (5{,}445 parameters). (Course figure IW\nobreakspace {}10.1.)\relax }}{155}{figure.caption.100}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.1}{\ignorespaces Turning pixels into tokens. (a) At stage\nobreakspace {}1 a \texttt {Conv2d} with kernel\nobreakspace {}4 and stride\nobreakspace {}4 summarises each $4\times 4$ block of the 32-channel input as one 32-number token (amber: one block and its token), giving a $32\times 32$ token grid that is flattened to 1{,}024 tokens. (b) Stage\nobreakspace {}1's windows tile exactly, so each pixel feeds one token, which hiding requires; stages 2 to 4 use kernel\nobreakspace {}3, stride\nobreakspace {}2, padding\nobreakspace {}1, whose windows overlap by one. (Course figure IW\nobreakspace {}11.1.)\relax }}{170}{figure.caption.104}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14.1}{\ignorespaces The attention matrix $\mathbf {A}$ of \cref {we:attention:full}, drawn as a heat map (darker plum = larger weight). Row $i$ is how query $i$ shares its attention among the three keys; every row sums to 1, columns need not. Token 3, which resembles both others, puts half its attention on itself.\relax }}{184}{figure.caption.108}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14.2}{\ignorespaces The same example as the course draws it: (a) three 2-number tokens with $\mathbf {Q}=\mathbf {K}=\mathbf {V}=\mathbf {X}$; (b) scaled scores $\mathbf {q}\cdot \mathbf {k}/\sqrt 2$; (c) softmax along each row; (d) each output is the weighted average of the values. Every number matches \cref {we:attention:full}.\relax }}{184}{figure.caption.109}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14.3}{\ignorespaces Where the questions come from. (a) In self-attention the tokens produce queries, keys and values. (b) In cross-attention an outside set of queries reads the tokens' keys and values. Right: the two score matrices for the spectral case of \cref {ch:ceiling}, drawn to scale.\relax }}{190}{figure.caption.112}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15.1}{\ignorespaces The toy reduction of \cref {we:esa:toyconv} with the averaging kernel. Each output token summarises one $R\times R$ block of the input (outlined); the queries are still made from all 16 input tokens.\relax }}{196}{figure.caption.114}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15.2}{\ignorespaces Spatial reduction at stage 1, as the course draws it. Queries keep all 1{,}024 tokens; keys and values are first shrunk by a strided convolution (kernel = stride = $R = 8$) to a $4\times 4$ grid of 16 summary tokens. The score matrix is $1024\times 16$ instead of $1024\times 1024$, 64 times smaller (the hatched square is not drawn to scale). With $R = 8, 4, 2, 1$ every stage attends over the same $4\times 4$ summary.\relax }}{198}{figure.caption.115}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15.3}{\ignorespaces Score entries per head per patch, plain against spatially reduced, on a logarithmic axis (each gridline is ten times the one below). The reduction factor $R^2$ is 64, 16, 4 and 1.\relax }}{199}{figure.caption.116}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.1}{\ignorespaces The feed-forward half of a block. Tokens are expanded fourfold, reshaped back to their grid, mixed with their eight neighbours by a depthwise $3\times 3$ convolution (one private filter per channel), passed through GELU and projected back. The depthwise convolution is what gives the model a sense of position.\relax }}{210}{figure.caption.122}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.2}{\ignorespaces GELU against ReLU. GELU is a smooth ReLU: near zero for very negative inputs, a soft bend through the origin, near-linear for large inputs. The curve is drawn with the tanh approximation, which differs from the exact form by less than 0.001 on this range.\relax }}{213}{figure.caption.123}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.3}{\ignorespaces One SegFormer block. Each of the two sublayers, efficient self-attention (plum) and Mix-FFN (indigo), normalises its input first (pre-norm), transforms it, applies dropout and adds the result back to its input through a residual connection. The shape $(B, N, C)$ is unchanged; each encoder stage stacks two blocks.\relax }}{214}{figure.caption.124}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.1}{\ignorespaces The encoder pyramid drawn to scale. Each square covers the same 128 $\times $ 128 pixels of ground; its grid lines are the stage's tokens (stage 1: one token per 4 $\times $ 4 pixels, stage 4: one per 32 $\times $ 32). The bar under each square is the token width, drawn at one scale (256 channels = the full square width). Grey cells in F1 are hidden tokens at a 65\% hiding rate (an illustrative pattern); only F1 has them. Arrows are patch embeddings (stride 2, kernel 3).\relax }}{221}{figure.caption.127}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.2}{\ignorespaces The course's view of the same pyramid: each patch embedding (arrow) shrinks the grid, by 4 at stage 1 and by 2 after, and widens the channels, producing F1 to F4 at 1/4 to 1/32 of the input resolution. Hidden tokens (hatched) exist at stage 1 only. Front-face size and depth are compressed for drawing; \cref {fig:encoder:pyramid} is to scale.\relax }}{221}{figure.caption.128}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.1}{\ignorespaces Token validity on an 8 $\times $ 8 pixel mask with the real stage-1 geometry (kernel 4, stride 4). (a) The pixel mask split into four 4 $\times $ 4 blocks. (b) Average pooling gives each block's valid fraction: 16/16, 4/16, 12/16, 8/16. (c) The strict threshold $> 0.5$ gives the token mask $[1, 0, 1, 0]$; the exactly-half block is invalid.\relax }}{231}{figure.caption.132}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.2}{\ignorespaces The worked example of \cref {we:masking:plus2} as one picture. Invalid tokens (hatched) get $+2$ so they sort to the back. After an ascending sort the first $\lfloor 4 \times 0.5 \rfloor = 2$ places (amber) are flagged and scattered back: tokens 0 and 4 are hidden, and the keep mask is the exact complement of the prediction mask.\relax }}{236}{figure.caption.135}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.3}{\ignorespaces Hidden tokens are removed, not zeroed. The gather keeps only the visible tokens (here 4 of 6), so the stage-1 blocks never see the hidden ones; after the blocks the scatter writes the encoded tokens back into a zero tensor at their original positions.\relax }}{239}{figure.caption.137}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.4}{\ignorespaces Erosion as a max pool, with the 1-D example of \cref {we:masking:erode1d} (kernel 5). Flipping makes invalid pixels 1; a stride-1 max pool grows that region by $\lfloor 5/2 \rfloor = 2$ pixels on each side; flipping back leaves the valid region shrunk by 2 pixels per edge.\relax }}{243}{figure.caption.140}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.5}{\ignorespaces The four masks on one 16 $\times $ 16 patch with 4 $\times $ 4 tokens, in the book's colours (indigo spatial tokens, amber loss, grey invalid). A cloud (dashed) covers rows 10--15, columns 11--15. (a) Tokens 11 and 15 are invalid (valid fractions $8/16$ and $0/16$); the other 14 are valid and $\lfloor 0.65 \times 14 \rfloor = 9$ of them are hidden (an illustrative draw). (b) The loss mask with erosion radius 1 (kernel 3): hidden pixels count only when valid and not next to the cloud. Worked in \cref {we:masking:fourmasks}.\relax }}{243}{figure.caption.141}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19.1}{\ignorespaces The decoder. The four encoder maps are projected to 256 channels by 1 $\times $ 1 convolutions, resized to F1's 32 $\times $ 32, concatenated to 1,024 channels, fused and refined, and expanded to $512 = 32 \times 4 \times 4$ channels. PixelShuffle(4) rearranges each group of 16 channels into a 4 $\times $ 4 block of pixels (inset, with $r = 2$), returning \texttt {(B, 32, 128, 128)} without any arithmetic.\relax }}{252}{figure.caption.143}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19.2}{\ignorespaces The seven steps of \texttt {forward()} for a batch of four 128 $\times $ 128 patches. Spectral steps are teal, spatial steps indigo. Step 4 expands into the four encoder stages (hiding at stage 1 only). The model stops at the reconstruction; the trainer computes the loss (amber) in raw reflectance.\relax }}{255}{figure.caption.146}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20.1}{\ignorespaces Where Indradhanu's 5,506,629 parameters live, drawn to scale from the counts of this chapter. Spectral layers teal, encoder indigo, decoder plum. Stage 4's two blocks (16 tokens) hold more than any other component; the decoder's two refine convolutions hold 1,770,240 between them; the spectral compressor and decompressor are invisible at this scale (0.2\% together).\relax }}{262}{figure.caption.153}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20.2}{\ignorespaces The course's view of the same budget by component, bar widths to scale: the blocks (51.4\%) and decoder (39.3\%) carry almost all of it.\relax }}{263}{figure.caption.154}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21.1}{\ignorespaces Chakshu's forward pass on a masked batch of eight $256\times 256$ Landsat patches, with the shape after each box (recorded by forward hooks on the real model \,{\footnotesize \leavevmode {\color {gray}[C]}}). Blue: encoder; plum: decoder; amber: the two ends, in degrees Celsius. The chapters on the left explain each box.\relax }}{274}{figure.caption.160}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21.2}{\ignorespaces Where Chakshu's 406,496 parameters live, drawn to scale from \cref {we:chakshu:blocks} and the sums above (blue encoder, plum decoder). The largest single layer is the decoder's refine convolution, 20\% of the model; the stage-4 patch embedding (55,584) alone outweighs the first three blocks together (stage-end norms, 416, not drawn).\relax }}{276}{figure.caption.161}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21.3}{\ignorespaces An $8\times 8$-pixel window around the $3\times 3$ spot of \cref {sec:chakshu:straddle}. Thick lines are stage-1 token borders. In pass 1 the grey tokens are hidden, but the two teal tokens still show four of the spot's nine hot pixels to the encoder, so the hidden hot pixels are predicted warm; pass 2 swaps the colours.\relax }}{279}{figure.caption.162}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22.1}{\ignorespaces The ceiling of a fixed compressor. (a) Every pixel -- forest, desert or shadow -- is mixed by the same trained matrix $\mathbf {W}$. (b) A dead band filled with 0.0 still receives its weight: with the toy numbers the output moves from $-0.50$ to $-0.30$ (\cref {we:ceiling:dead}).\relax }}{289}{figure.caption.164}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22.2}{\ignorespaces Shapes in cross-attention. The token count $N$ is an inner size in both products, so it never reaches the output: $M$ queries always give $M$ answers.\relax }}{290}{figure.caption.166}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22.3}{\ignorespaces Where the questions come from, and what it costs. (a) In self-attention the band tokens produce queries, keys and values. (b) In cross-attention an outside set of $D$ queries reads the tokens' keys and values. Right: score matrices for one pixel and head with 202 bands, drawn to scale: 40,804 against 6,464 scores.\relax }}{293}{figure.caption.171}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22.4}{\ignorespaces The cosine similarity of two wavelength tags, \cref {eq:ceiling:cossim}, plotted exactly from the sixteen periods of the source. It starts at 1 for $\Delta = 0$, is still about 0.6 at 20--30\,nm, and beyond about 100\,nm it fluctuates around a mean of about 0.06, never above 0.49 nor below $-0.35$: tags of distant wavelengths are close to orthogonal. The two marked points are \cref {we:ceiling:cossim}.\relax }}{298}{figure.caption.173}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22.5}{\ignorespaces Building one band token. (a) The band's value is stretched to $d$ numbers by \texttt {Linear(1, d)} and added to a fixed sinusoidal tag of its wavelength. (b) The real 32-number tag for three wavelengths (teal positive, amber negative, shade = magnitude): 660 and 670\,nm share most of their pattern (cosine similarity 0.671), 660 and 1650\,nm almost none (0.013).\relax }}{299}{figure.caption.174}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23.1}{\ignorespaces The per-band cross-attention compressor for one pixel. Each of 202 bands becomes a token; keys and values are projected from the tokens; 32 learned queries, shared by every pixel, attend over them with 4 heads, and each answer becomes one output channel. None of the 5,345 parameters depends on the band count.\relax }}{303}{figure.caption.176}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23.2}{\ignorespaces Minus infinity before softmax. Left: three valid scores $[2, 1, 0]$ give weights $[0.6652, 0.2447, 0.0900]$. Middle: marking band 3 dead sets its score to $-\infty $, its weight to exactly 0, and the other two renormalise to $[0.7311, 0.2689]$. Right: the same mask lets a 202-band SpectralEarth pixel share a batch with 239-band PRISMA pixels, its 37 padding tokens masked out.\relax }}{309}{figure.caption.179}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23.3}{\ignorespaces Why training, not inference, hits the wall. (a) For backpropagation the per-band stem keeps about 19 intermediates of shape (16,384 pixels, 202 bands, 32), 202 MiB each in bf16: about 3.75 GiB per $128\times 128$ patch, matching the measured 3.7 GiB. (b) The reduced stem of \cref {ch:specdecoder} never builds a band-by-32 tensor; its largest per-pixel tensor has 53 bin slots, 53 MiB.\relax }}{314}{figure.caption.181}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23.4}{\ignorespaces Memory per patch of the quantities computed in this section, to scale (linear axis). All are this book's arithmetic from the shapes; the training bar is $19\times 202$ MiB, matching the measured 3.7\,GiB. The self-attention bar, 9.96\,GiB, is why the design is cross-attention.\relax }}{315}{figure.caption.182}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24.1}{\ignorespaces The decoder turned around. The pixel's 32 latent channels, each stretched and given a learned identity, supply keys and values; every requested wavelength becomes a query through its tag. Attention and a final Linear(32, 1) produce one reflectance per requested wavelength, so the same 6,401 parameters reconstruct any band list.\relax }}{320}{figure.caption.183}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24.2}{\ignorespaces The trace in one row. The new spectral parts (plum) and wavelength normalisation work strictly inside each pixel; hiding, encoder and decoder are the unchanged spatial model; the loss counts valid bands only. Because hidden pixels are removed before any spatial attention, a pixel cannot rebuild its own spectrum.\relax }}{323}{figure.caption.185}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24.3}{\ignorespaces Variant B. A small network reads each pixel's own spectrum and emits one score per band; sigmoid turns the scores into gates between 0 and 1, the bands are scaled by them, and today's fixed 1$\times $1 compressor mixes the result. Each pixel therefore gets the recipe $\mathbf {W}\operatorname {diag}(\mathbf {g})$, today's $\mathbf {W}$ with rescaled columns, at three times today's arithmetic.\relax }}{328}{figure.caption.188}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24.4}{\ignorespaces SpectralEarth's 202 bands counted into the 53 fixed 40\,nm bins, to scale, from the real wavelength table (this book's script). Grey crosses mark the 11 empty bins, masked out of attention. The two bins of 9 (920--1000\,nm) are where EnMAP's two detectors overlap.\relax }}{331}{figure.caption.192}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24.5}{\ignorespaces The fixed bin grid as the course draws it. The 400 to 2500\,nm axis is cut into 53 bins of 40\,nm (top); each tick below is one of SpectralEarth's 202 band centres, and the bar height is the number of bands in that bin (1 to 9). 42 bins hold at least one band; the 11 empty bins (hatched) fall in the two water-vapour gaps and at the long end.\relax }}{331}{figure.caption.193}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24.6}{\ignorespaces The reduced compressor for one pixel. Left: band values (teal) are masked by validity (grey hatching marks a dead band) and multiplied by a per-band weight vector $k(\lambda )$ computed from each band's wavelength; the products are averaged within each 40\,nm bin and given the bin centre's tag, giving about 42 bin tokens. Centre: 32 learned queries attend over the bin tokens (plum), empty bins masked, and each answer becomes one channel. Bottom: the skip path multiplies every valid band by $W(\lambda )$ and averages, the 1$\times $1 compressor with wavelength-computed columns. The two halves add into the 32-channel latent $z$.\relax }}{335}{figure.caption.194}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24.7}{\ignorespaces The wavelength-linear decoder. Each requested wavelength (any list: 202 SpectralEarth, 224 EnMAP, 239 PRISMA bands) passes through one small network that emits a 32-number weight row $U(\lambda )$ and a bias $c(\lambda )$; the band's value is the dot product of that row with the pixel's latent $z$, plus the bias. No attention and no per-band stored weights: the same 4,257 parameters serve every band list.\relax }}{336}{figure.caption.195}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24.8}{\ignorespaces The L40S measurements of the drift box, as bars on linear axes (to scale). Left: training throughput; the per-band stem is the short plum bars, the reduced stem the dark plum. Right: peak memory. ``OOM'': the per-band stem ran out of memory at batch 32.\relax }}{339}{figure.caption.198}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24.9}{\ignorespaces The same measurements as the course draws them. Left: patches per second against batch size (log scale). Right: peak memory. The per-band cross-attention stem runs out of memory at batch 32; the reduced stem is about six times faster at each stem's best batch and needs about a quarter of the memory per added patch.\relax }}{339}{figure.caption.199}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25.1}{\ignorespaces Which pixels are graded. The pixel prediction mask (hidden tokens, amber) is multiplied by the eroded validity mask (indigo; the cloud and its collar hatched). Multiplying 0/1 masks is a logical AND, so only pixels that were hidden, valid and away from invalid regions enter the loss. Right: the L1 term for one graded pixel.\relax }}{346}{figure.caption.201}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25.2}{\ignorespaces Two complementary errors. (a) Treating a spectrum as a vector, the spectral angle is the angle between reconstruction and truth, and L1 the average per-band distance. A reconstruction that is the truth scaled by 2 (amber) has an angle of exactly 0 but a large L1; a reconstruction close in level but slightly rotated (indigo) has a small L1 and a small angle. (b) The three-band numbers of \cref {we:losses:sam}.\relax }}{354}{figure.caption.207}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25.3}{\ignorespaces The size of the derivative of arccos, drawn to scale from $1/\sqrt {1-u^2}$, on a logarithmic vertical axis. Dots are the four table rows. (a) Against $u$ itself: flat until about $u = 0.9$, then a wall. (b) Against $1 - u$ on a log axis, reading right to left as $u \to 1$: a straight line of slope $-\frac 12$, because $1/\sqrt {1-u^2} \approx 1/\sqrt {2(1-u)}$ there. The shaded band, $u \ge 0.999$, is where a well-trained model's pixels sit.\relax }}{356}{figure.caption.212}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25.4}{\ignorespaces The SAM-weight ramp of both shipped configurations, drawn to scale from \cref {eq:losses:ramp}. The weight is held for a whole epoch, so each ramp is a staircase; the thin amber line is the formula $\lambda = 0.05\,t$ that the stairs follow. Both configurations happen to rise at the same rate, 0.05 per epoch; v0.2.0 stops at half the height. After the ramp both stay flat for the rest of training (to $t = 199$ or 499).\relax }}{362}{figure.caption.218}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25.5}{\ignorespaces The course's drawing of the v0.2.0 ramp: the weight on the angle rises linearly from 0 at epoch index 0 to 0.5 at epoch index 10 and then stays flat, so L1 leads early training and the angle is phased in.\relax }}{362}{figure.caption.219}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26.1}{\ignorespaces The learning-rate schedules of v0.2.0 and F001}}{374}{figure.caption.226}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26.2}{\ignorespaces The training-data pipeline, one job per stage. A raw scene becomes a 165-band vendable; PatchPlanGenerator decides where to cut 128 $\times $ 128 patches (stride 64 in training, so neighbours overlap by half, the last snapping to the scene edge); the patcher cuts them; they are packed into .tar shards on S3 and streamed by the DataLoader as batches.\relax }}{377}{figure.caption.228}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26.3}{\ignorespaces The training loop of \texttt {FoundationTrainer} with Indradhanu's loss, as the source runs it. The left column is one training step, repeated until the epoch's sample budget is spent; the right column runs once per epoch: validation, the scheduler step, logging and the periodic checkpoint. The SAM weight $\lambda (t)$ and the learning rate $\eta (t)$ both change only at the epoch boundary.\relax }}{381}{figure.caption.231}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26.4}{\ignorespaces The course's drawing of one epoch: a training pass with random masking and gradient accumulation, a validation pass, a scheduler step, logging, and a periodic checkpoint. The dashed frame repeats once per epoch.\relax }}{381}{figure.caption.232}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {27.1}{\ignorespaces The four epochs of \cref {we:pretrain:ramp} scored two ways. With the training ramp (amber, dashed) the validation score climbs from 0.055 to 0.099 although L1 and SAM both fall; epoch 1 stays ``best'' for ever. At the full SAM weight (teal) the score falls from 0.155 to 0.099. The two meet at epoch 11, where the ramp ends.\relax }}{389}{figure.caption.239}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {27.2}{\ignorespaces Left: how each sensor's bands divide between the visible, near-infrared and shortwave infrared. PRISMA's native list gives the visible 16\% of its bands against SpectralEarth's 26\%. Right: E004's error on water, on SpectralEarth test patches with all 202 bands, on the same pixels thinned to PRISMA's spacing, and with the skip path reweighted at inference.\relax }}{392}{figure.caption.242}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {27.3}{\ignorespaces (a) The ten 30,000-patch blocks along F001's 200 epochs, under the one cosine learning-rate curve that runs through all of them. (b) The loop in \texttt {rotate\_f001.sh}: every decision is computed from \texttt {metrics.csv} and two marker files, so the script can be killed and restarted at any point.\relax }}{396}{figure.caption.247}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {27.4}{\ignorespaces F001's records drawn to scale from its README (validation rows at epochs 6, 13, 20, 38, 60, 109; bench at 7, 14, 20, 38, 60, 109). (a) Validation L1 falls at every checkpoint until epoch 60 and then flattens. (b) The bench under the \texttt {combined} score jumps above production's 0.662 by epoch 20 and then stays flat; residual Mahalanobis, measured at epochs 60 and 109, sits about 0.13 higher. (c) Validation SAM follows L1.\relax }}{398}{figure.caption.250}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {27.5}{\ignorespaces The course's version of the same records, including the residual-Mahalanobis measurement at epoch 52 (0.865) reported in the scoring comparison of 2026-10-15.\relax }}{399}{figure.caption.251}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {27.6}{\ignorespaces (a) The three shard sets as they stood on 2026-10-16 (colours identify the sensor only). (b) The one rule that lets mixed band lists share a trainer: match by wavelength within 0.5\,nm; a target band with no match becomes nodata, a source band with no target is dropped.\relax }}{400}{figure.caption.252}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28.1}{\ignorespaces The head in context: the shared stem and encoder feed two heads. Pass 1 removes 65\% of the tokens and reconstructs; pass 2 keeps every token and segments. Which passes run depends on the training mode (\cref {ch:finetune}).\relax }}{408}{figure.caption.264}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28.2}{\ignorespaces The worked example. Hatched cells are unknown and contribute nothing. Cloud, unknown on a dry scene, drops out of the class average; the loss is the mean of water's and shadow's terms.\relax }}{418}{figure.caption.269}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28.3}{\ignorespaces (a) Share of cirrus labels that are surface-driven, by the scene's water vapour, for cirrus level 1 and level 2 or more; below 0.75\,g/cm$^2$ (dashed) nearly all are. (b) Candidate policies: share of suspect labels each removes against share of plausible labels it also removes. The adopted rule removes 62.6\% of suspect labels for 13.6\% of plausible ones.\relax }}{421}{figure.caption.271}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29.1}{\ignorespaces (a) The six stages, what each trains, and the gate (amber) it must pass; stage 4 is optional. (b) Batches are drawn by fixed shares (top), not in proportion to each sensor's tile count (bottom), which would give AVIRIS-NG 43\% of training.\relax }}{425}{figure.caption.277}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29.2}{\ignorespaces (a) The last micro-batches of an epoch, one sensor each, and the running mean they drag around; validation uses wherever it ends. (b) In training each sensor is normalised by its own batch mean (top); at evaluation every sensor is normalised by the one running mean (bottom), so each arrives with an offset the encoder never saw.\relax }}{431}{figure.caption.285}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29.3}{\ignorespaces Three sensors, three stem passes (teal), one concatenated batch through the single BatchNorm, encoder and decoder (indigo), split by row ranges and decoded at each sensor's own wavelengths.\relax }}{434}{figure.caption.289}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29.4}{\ignorespaces One mixed batch of 8 tiles goes through the shared BatchNorm and encoder twice: masked, to the decoder, for the reconstruction loss on all 8; unmasked, to the segmentation head, for the segmentation loss on the 3 EnMAP tiles only (the other 5 rows are normalised but not scored). Total loss $=$ reconstruction $+ 0.1 \times $ segmentation.\relax }}{437}{figure.caption.290}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30.1}{\ignorespaces Two-pass checkerboard inference (course figure 29.1). Each token's final value is taken from the pass that hid it, so the combined reconstruction is built entirely from predictions. See the source check below for which pass hides the top-left token.\relax }}{447}{figure.caption.297}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30.2}{\ignorespaces Sliding-window inference (course figure 29.2). $128\times 128$ tiles start every 32 pixels, so a $1000\times 1000$ scene needs $29\times 29=841$ tiles; an interior pixel lies in $128/32=4$ tiles along each axis and so in $4\times 4=16$ tiles in all.\relax }}{451}{figure.caption.298}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30.3}{\ignorespaces Tiles covering each column of a 1000-pixel axis}}{452}{figure.caption.299}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30.4}{\ignorespaces The \texttt {combined} scoring method (course figure 30.1): per-pixel L1 and SAM maps are each divided by their maximum over the valid pixels and blended with weight 0.5. Since 2026-10-15 this is no longer Indradhanu's default.\relax }}{455}{figure.caption.304}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30.5}{\ignorespaces The product's action chain for a hyperspectral scene (AD13). Teal steps transform spectra, plum runs the model, amber is where a person decides.\relax }}{457}{figure.caption.305}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31.1}{\ignorespaces Rotate, divide, measure (course figure 45.1, the course's two-band version of the example above). (a) Ordinary residuals (grey) spread along the shared diagonal far more than across it; one test pixel lies further out along the diagonal, the other across it. (b) The same points turned onto the eigenvectors. (c) After dividing by each direction's spread the ordinary pixels sit on one circle and the across-the-diagonal pixel is far outside it.\relax }}{466}{figure.caption.307}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31.2}{\ignorespaces Size versus direction (anomaly course figure 12.3). Left: ordinary residuals spread widely along direction 1 and barely along direction 2. Right: after whitening the cloud is round; the pixel far out along the common direction is inside it, the one a short way along the rare direction is far outside.\relax }}{467}{figure.caption.308}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31.3}{\ignorespaces The ring, and a box sum from four look-ups (course figure 46.1). (a) The $61\times 61$ window minus the hatched $21\times 21$ guard leaves a ring of 3,280 pixels whose mean is subtracted from the centre pixel. (b) A $4\times 4$ grid and its zero-led integral image: the shaded $2\times 2$ box sums to 6 from four entries of the table.\relax }}{470}{figure.caption.311}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31.4}{\ignorespaces The comparison drawn to scale from the table (F001, epoch 52, 2026-10-15). Amber: scores built on the residual's size; teal: scores built on its direction (C, D and the filters applied to C). The axis starts at 0.65 to separate the bars; the gap from A to C is $0.849-0.715=0.134$.\relax }}{476}{figure.caption.313}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31.5}{\ignorespaces The same comparison as a dot plot (course figure 46.2), with object counts where they were recorded.\relax }}{476}{figure.caption.314}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31.6}{\ignorespaces Per-scene ROC-AUC of A, C and D, to scale (F001, epoch 52). D beats C on every scene; the axis starts at 0.6.\relax }}{477}{figure.caption.315}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {32.1}{\ignorespaces Before 2026-10-31, comparing the two Indradhanu models on one scene took two branches, each with its own band filter and scoring action. Since then one native band filter and one scoring action serve both. A classical detector can ride along in either.\relax }}{493}{figure.caption.324}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {33.1}{\ignorespaces The ROC of the six pixels of \cref {we:measures:six} (indigo staircase), the chance diagonal (grey, dashed), and the region counted by a partial AUC with limit $\mathrm {FPR} \le 0.5$ (amber). Drawn to scale from the worked numbers. The full area under the staircase is $7/9$; the amber area is $5/18$, which normalised by the limit gives $5/9$ (\cref {we:measures:pauc}).\relax }}{501}{figure.caption.327}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {33.2}{\ignorespaces The course's figure for this chapter. (a) The five-pixel ROC of the course's own example (targets at 0.9 and 0.7 among five pixels), AUC $5/6$. (b) Detectors A and B: B has the larger total area; inside the first 1\% of false alarms (the amber strip, stretched, not to scale) A is twenty times higher.\relax }}{503}{figure.caption.330}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {33.3}{\ignorespaces Detectors A (teal) and B (indigo, dashed) of \cref {we:measures:ab}, drawn to scale. Left: the whole curve, where B's larger area comes from FPR 0.01--0.30. Right: the first 1.2\% of false alarms, where A's area (amber, up to FPR 0.01) is twenty times B's. The chance diagonal (grey) is nearly flat on the right-hand scale.\relax }}{504}{figure.caption.331}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {33.4}{\ignorespaces Left: a two-pixel object (amber) and the six pixels its 1-pixel tolerance adds (indigo); a flagged pixel anywhere in these $m = 8$ cells catches it. Right: two target pixels touching only at a corner are two objects under 4-connectivity (the book's script confirms \texttt {ndimage.label} returns 2).\relax }}{508}{figure.caption.335}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {34.1}{\ignorespaces What each benchmark supplies: the four things a model needs, plus platform. Amber cells are the reason a benchmark cannot test this product as it is.\relax }}{514}{figure.caption.342}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {34.2}{\ignorespaces The diamond ring around a one-pixel object. Distance 1 (grey) is skipped; distances 2 (8 pixels) and 3 (12 pixels) form the 20-pixel ring (teal). The dashed square shows the 40-pixel ring a $3 \times 3$ dilation would have made.\relax }}{515}{figure.caption.344}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {34.3}{\ignorespaces The mixing line of \cref {we:benchmarks:mix} in the plane of two bands: every mixture of background $\mathbf {b}$ and material $\mathbf {t}$ lies on the straight segment between them, at a distance from $\mathbf {b}$ proportional to the abundance $a$.\relax }}{517}{figure.caption.348}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {34.4}{\ignorespaces $z(s)$ for \cref {we:benchmarks:solve}. The simple formula's $s = 0.5$ lands at $z = 0.6$ (medium); the solver follows the curve through its minimum at $s = 0.2$ and crosses the target at $s = 0.7$.\relax }}{519}{figure.caption.351}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {34.5}{\ignorespaces The abundance map of \cref {we:benchmarks:psf}: a one-pixel bench-v2 implant at strength 0.1 with a Gaussian PSF of $\sigma = 1.4$, shown on its $3 \times 3$ footprint (darker amber = more material). The blur continues beyond the window, weaker with distance.\relax }}{522}{figure.caption.353}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {34.6}{\ignorespaces pAUC to 1\% false alarms for FT03 (amber) and PCA-RX (teal) on three benchmarks. The real PRISMA bench puts FT03 first; bench-v1 reversed that order; bench-v2 restores it.\relax }}{524}{figure.caption.358}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {34.7}{\ignorespaces What bench-v2 had to match: medians on PRISMA, real objects (grey) against bench-v1 implants (amber), overall and in the easy tier, on the diagnosis ruler; the shaded band is within 25\% of the real value.\relax }}{524}{figure.caption.359}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {35.1}{\ignorespaces pAUC to 1\% false alarms. On implant bench-v1 (a) the model-free PCA-RX (teal) is highest; on the real PRISMA bench (b) it is lowest, and excluding water and cloud (hatched) helps the model on both.\relax }}{532}{figure.caption.367}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {35.2}{\ignorespaces The 39 labelled objects lying wholly inside FT03's water / cloud mask, by kind. Excluding water and cloud hides all 39 (amber); excluding cloud only hides the 4 in set4 that sit under haze FT03 calls cloud.\relax }}{534}{figure.caption.370}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {36.1}{\ignorespaces The exact bootstrap distribution of the mean of five numbers: every one of the $5^5 = 3{,}125$ draws with replacement, counted by the mean it gives (computed by \texttt {research/textbook/tools/compute\_bootstrap.py}). The teal band is the percentile 95\% interval. The distribution is lumpy because only 43 distinct means are possible, and skewed right because one value (12) is far from the rest.\relax }}{540}{figure.caption.377}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {36.2}{\ignorespaces The paired-bootstrap intervals of \cref {tab:bootstrap:results}, drawn to scale: point value (dot) and 95\% interval (bar) of the pAUC difference $B - A$. Teal: interval wholly above zero (a resolved gain); amber: wholly below zero (a resolved loss); grey: crosses zero (a tie). FT09b's interval clears zero by 0.0002.\relax }}{545}{figure.caption.383}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {37.1}{\ignorespaces pAUC by training stage, as read on 2026-10-17: the implanted bench (amber, easy and medium tiers) rises from v0.2.0 to FT02c, the real bench (grey) wanders with no order. The v0.2.0 points are the bug this chapter dissects.\relax }}{550}{figure.caption.385}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {37.2}{\ignorespaces The same three samples (500, 600, 700\,nm; values 1, 2, 4). (a) Listed ascending, \texttt {np.interp} draws the line: 1.5 at 550\,nm and 3.0 at 650\,nm. (b) Listed descending, it returns the last listed value, 1, for every wavelength above 500\,nm: the spectrum goes flat.\relax }}{553}{figure.caption.386}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {37.3}{\ignorespaces Objects caught at the top 1\% on the three implant benches, drawn to scale. The wavelength-reading models (F001, FT03) and PCA-RX move a few points with each rebuild; v0.2.0, whose reading was the only one fixed, jumps 13.4 points from bench-v2 to bench-v2.1. Its bench-v1 and bench-v2 bars measured a broken resample.\relax }}{558}{figure.caption.394}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38.1}{\ignorespaces FT01's IoU by epoch, drawn to scale from \cref {tab:ftruns:ft01}. Water, cloud and shadow climb; thin cloud (grey) stays between 0.06 and 0.09 for the whole run.\relax }}{565}{figure.caption.402}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38.2}{\ignorespaces FT02's bench by epoch against the gate. Residual Mahalanobis (amber) starts above the 0.861 gate at epoch 1 and settles below it; \texttt {combined} (grey) climbs from F001's 0.706 to 0.824. best.pt is epoch 8, chosen by validation reconstruction, not by the bench.\relax }}{566}{figure.caption.404}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38.3}{\ignorespaces (a) Edge-strength correction (amber) pushes down everything on strong edges, the field boundary and the target alike. (b) Under the blind spot: a boundary passing through a hidden token (grey hatching) can be continued from its visible ends; a target wholly inside the hidden token leaves no trace in the context.\relax }}{570}{figure.caption.411}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38.4}{\ignorespaces The four options discussed after FT02, what each would add, its main cost, and where it stood.\relax }}{572}{figure.caption.416}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38.5}{\ignorespaces Medians on PRISMA, real objects (grey) against bench-v1 implants (amber), overall and in the easy tier; the shaded band is within 25\% of the real value.\relax }}{574}{figure.caption.425}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38.6}{\ignorespaces One FT05 step. The loader workers (CPU) implant half the tiles and keep the clean copy; the model (GPU) reads the implanted tile and is graded against the clean one, $\times 5$ near the implants. The CPU side sets the pace.\relax }}{577}{figure.caption.429}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38.7}{\ignorespaces Share of objects caught at the top 1\% on the real bench (grey) and bench-v2.1 (amber), drawn to scale from \cref {tab:ftruns:real} and \cref {tab:sortbug:v21}. Every model is far above chance and above PCA-RX; the models tie each other.\relax }}{580}{figure.caption.432}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38.8}{\ignorespaces The course's version of the same board: share caught at the top 1\% for each model and PCA-RX, on the real bench (grey) and bench-v2.1 (amber).\relax }}{580}{figure.caption.433}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38.9}{\ignorespaces (a) How one teacher probability becomes a target: taught ``yes'' above 0.9, ``no'' below 0.1, nothing between, and a confident ``water'' over a bright NIR turned into a known not-water. (b) The real-bench pAUC from the shipped v0.2.0 to the release candidate: the model adds 0.003, cloud exclusion 0.008.\relax }}{584}{figure.caption.438}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {39.1}{\ignorespaces The worker's path for Indradhanu V2, in order. Every box is one of the decisions of this chapter; the parity run (\cref {sec:shipping:parity}) runs the same boxes outside the worker and compares with the evaluator.\relax }}{595}{figure.caption.446}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {40.1}{\ignorespaces Pixel pAUC against objects caught, real PRISMA bench, drawn to scale from \cref {tab:scoringgains:results}. The dashed lines cross at plain RM. Nothing lies above the horizontal line: the A1 blurs (amber) move right and down - more pixel pAUC, fewer objects.\relax }}{599}{figure.caption.450}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {40.2}{\ignorespaces Per-scene pAUC of FT04 and its gated adaptations, drawn to scale. Set2 gains at every $N$; set1 and set4 lose at every $N$; nothing peaks at small $N$.\relax }}{611}{figure.caption.462}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {41.1}{\ignorespaces The Laplace density for three scales, drawn to scale from \cref {eq:uncertainty:laplace}, with a standard Gaussian in grey for comparison. The Laplace has a sharp peak at zero and heavier tails: a large error is less surprising under a Laplace than under a Gaussian of similar width.\relax }}{616}{figure.caption.465}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {41.2}{\ignorespaces The per-pixel loss of \cref {eq:uncertainty:loss} for $\left \lvert r \right \rvert =0.2$, drawn to scale, with the three points of \cref {we:uncertainty:three}. The curve rises steeply to the left (overconfidence) and slowly to the right (vagueness).\relax }}{618}{figure.caption.466}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {41.3}{\ignorespaces The three pixels of \cref {we:uncertainty:divide}, drawn to scale. Dividing by $b$ with a target/ring ratio of 1.19 drops the target below an ordinary flat-field pixel; the square root keeps it a little above.\relax }}{627}{figure.caption.473}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {42.1}{\ignorespaces The top-left token of a hidden $2\times 2$ block (thick outlines are blocks). Three of its 8 neighbours are in its own block and are certainly hidden; the other 5 lie in three neighbouring blocks.\relax }}{633}{figure.caption.476}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {42.2}{\ignorespaces The progressive decoder. The stem output reaches the decoder twice: through the encoder (top) and through the masked skip (bottom), at half and full resolution. Channel counts are written as ``channels @ size''.\relax }}{640}{figure.caption.487}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {42.3}{\ignorespaces \Cref {tab:decoderwork:cumulative} drawn to scale: point values with their 95\% paired-bootstrap intervals. Only cloud exclusion lies wholly right of zero; FT09b touches it, and FT09c falls back.\relax }}{645}{figure.caption.492}%
